<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kabir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Kabir Das</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kabir was an Indian mystic poet and saint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> whose verses influenced the Bhakti movement; some historians place his life in 1398–1448, others in 1440–1518. A few accounts say he was raised by a Muslim weaver and his wife, and he is believed to have been a disciple of Ramananda. His songs and couplets, composed orally and gathered in collections such as the Bijak, attacked the hypocrisy and misguided rituals he saw in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> alike. Verses attributed to him form the largest non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> contribution to the Adi Granth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kabir.jpg" alt="Kabir" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kabir004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
